Not exactly. A green verifier supports a narrower claim:

> **The generated code behaved correctly for every prompt-derived verifier case that was successfully bound and executed.**

That is not the same as:

> The code is fully compliant with the prompt.

## Why it is not proof of full compliance

### 1. The verifier is finite

It tests selected behaviors, not every requirement interaction, edge case, failure mode, or combination.

### 2. The verifier and writer use the same model

The verifier is extraction-independent because it sees only the prompt, not the documents. But it is not model-independent: Qwen writes the code and Qwen derives the cases. Shared interpretation blind spots can survive both.

### 3. Cases can still be wrong

A green run only means code matches the cases. If a case expectation is wrong, green is misleading. That is why confirmed/reviewed cases matter.

### 4. Prompt ambiguity remains

If the prompt does not specify behavior, the verifier cannot prove one implementation correct. Those cases must remain ambiguous or be resolved by a human.

### 5. Coverage is still incomplete

The requirement trace currently reports:

- 37 unmapped clauses;
- 12 partial clauses;
- 126 mapped-but-untested clauses;
- 170 mapped-and-tested clauses.

A green verifier would not make those disappear.

### 6. Non-behavioral requirements need other evidence

The verifier does not prove:

- performance;
- concurrency/atomicity beyond specific tests;
- security;
- usability;
- external integrations;
- persistence under crashes;
- every error path.

### 7. Green means no unbound cases either

A credible green state requires:

- zero failed cases;
- zero unbound cases;
- zero case defects;
- zero unresolved coverage gaps;
- all ambiguity decisions resolved;
- all requirement clauses traceable to at least one meaningful check.

## What you could claim if the verifier is green

With all cases confirmed and all requirements traced:

> The generated program is behaviorally compliant with the reviewed, prompt-derived test suite, with no observed discrepancies on the covered requirements.

That is a strong and defensible claim.

You could not yet claim:

> The program implements every requirement completely and correctly.

unless the unmapped and untested requirement clauses are also closed.

So the verifier is necessary evidence, but not sufficient proof of full prompt compliance. It is the behavioral gate, not the completeness proof.

